Myth: The Lighthouse of Quiet Light
Along a coastline where fog flowers bloom at dawn, there stood a lighthouse whose beam remembered. Its brickwork kept time with the tide, and its lantern room cradled a small mirror that reflected not images but names—of boats long gone, of storms survived, of friendships forged across gull-filled skies.

A quiet keeper named Kestrel tended the light and its mirror. Every evening she coaxed the ocean into a lullaby, and the lullaby stitched itself into the light, so that ships far offshore could read the names and find safe harbor. When the harbor fell silent, when nets came empty, Kestrel walked the shore with a basket of shells. She pressed shells into the sand, and the earth hummed back the memory of every harbor town that ever sheltered a traveler.

One night a child wandered to the cliff’s edge and whispered a wish for a new kind of harbor—one where memory becomes map and map becomes road home. The lighthouse answered by thinning the fog until a path of glow appeared: not a road, but a chorus of remembered journeys guiding all souls home to their own beginnings.
